/*
 * Help & handbook content for the Plan Illustrator.
 * Edit this file to add or change entries — no other code needs to change.
 *   GLOSSARY: { t: term, c: category, d: plain-language definition, x: example (optional),
 *               k: extra search words / synonyms (optional), r: related terms (optional) }
 *   HANDBOOK: { t: title, c: category, h: html body (trusted, written here) }
 * The product tables (ages, terms, riders, minimum premiums) are NOT written here: they are read live from the
 * app's own rate data, so they can never disagree with what the calculator does.
 * This is general guidance for agents. The company's product documents and the policy terms always prevail.
 */
window.HELP_CONTENT = {
  version: '1',
  categories: ['Core principles', 'People & parties', 'Premiums & payments', 'Benefits & values', 'Policy life-cycle', 'Riders & extra covers', 'Underwriting & claims', 'Types of life cover', 'Savings & investment', 'Regulation & compliance', 'Selling & conduct'],

  GLOSSARY: [
    /* ---------- Core principles ---------- */
    { t: 'Indemnity', c: 'Core principles', d: 'The principle that insurance should put the insured back in the same financial position as before the loss — no better, no worse. It applies to property and most general insurance (the insurer pays the actual loss, up to the limit). Life assurance is generally NOT a contract of indemnity, because the value of a life cannot be measured: a fixed sum assured is paid regardless of the financial loss.', x: 'A car worth KES 800,000 is stolen; an indemnity policy pays its market value (not a new car). A life policy with a KES 2,000,000 sum assured pays KES 2,000,000 on death.', k: 'indemnify compensation restore', r: ['Insurable interest', 'Sum assured', 'Subrogation', 'Contribution'] },
    { t: 'Insurable interest', c: 'Core principles', d: 'A legal or financial interest in the life or property insured, so that the person would suffer a real loss if the event happened. Without it the contract is not enforceable (it would be gambling). In life assurance it must exist when the policy starts. A person has unlimited insurable interest in their own life; others (spouse, dependent children, an employer in a key employee, a lender in a borrower) need to show a financial or close family interest.', x: 'A mother can insure her own life and name her child as beneficiary. A neighbour cannot insure someone they have no financial or family stake in.', k: 'interest stake', r: ['Utmost good faith', 'Indemnity', 'Beneficiary'] },
    { t: 'Utmost good faith', c: 'Core principles', d: 'Also called uberrimae fidei. Both the client and the insurer must be completely honest and disclose everything that could influence the decision to insure or the price. The client must disclose every material fact; the agent must explain the product truthfully and not mislead.', k: 'uberrimae fidei honesty disclosure', r: ['Material fact', 'Non-disclosure', 'Misrepresentation'] },
    { t: 'Material fact', c: 'Core principles', d: 'Any fact that would influence an insurer in deciding whether to accept a risk, or on what terms and price. Examples: age, health history, smoking, occupation, hazardous hobbies, existing cover, past declined applications.', r: ['Utmost good faith', 'Non-disclosure'] },
    { t: 'Non-disclosure', c: 'Core principles', d: 'Failing to tell the insurer a material fact. If discovered, the insurer may refuse a claim, change the terms or cancel the policy. Agents should help clients answer every proposal-form question completely and accurately.', r: ['Material fact', 'Misrepresentation', 'Contestability period'] },
    { t: 'Misrepresentation', c: 'Core principles', d: 'A false statement made when applying for cover — innocent (a genuine mistake), negligent or fraudulent. It can make the policy voidable or lead to a rejected claim.', r: ['Non-disclosure', 'Utmost good faith'] },
    { t: 'Proximate cause', c: 'Core principles', d: 'The dominant, effective cause of a loss — the one that sets the chain of events in motion. A claim is paid only if the proximate cause is an insured event and not an excluded one.', x: 'Death from complications of an accident is traced back to the accident; if accidental death is covered, the accident is the proximate cause.', r: ['Exclusion', 'Accidental Death Benefit'] },
    { t: 'Subrogation', c: 'Core principles', d: 'After paying a claim, the insurer steps into the insured’s shoes and may recover the loss from whoever caused it. It supports the principle of indemnity and applies to general insurance; it does not normally apply to life assurance payouts.', r: ['Indemnity', 'Contribution'] },
    { t: 'Contribution', c: 'Core principles', d: 'When the same risk is insured with more than one insurer under indemnity contracts, each insurer pays only its share of the loss, so the insured cannot profit by claiming the full amount twice. Not applicable to life policies, where several policies may each pay in full.', r: ['Indemnity', 'Subrogation', 'Double insurance'] },
    { t: 'Double insurance', c: 'Core principles', d: 'Insuring the same risk with two or more insurers. Under indemnity insurance the client can only recover the actual loss in total. With life cover a person may hold several policies and all pay their sum assured.', r: ['Contribution', 'Indemnity'] },
    { t: 'Warranty', c: 'Core principles', d: 'A promise by the insured that a statement is true or that something will (or will not) be done. Breach can allow the insurer to refuse a claim or end the cover.', r: ['Condition', 'Exclusion'] },
    { t: 'Condition', c: 'Core principles', d: 'A term of the policy that must be met by the insured (and sometimes the insurer) for the contract to operate — for example paying premiums on time or notifying a claim promptly.', r: ['Warranty', 'Exclusion'] },
    { t: 'Risk', c: 'Core principles', d: 'The possibility of a loss. In insurance only pure risks (loss or no loss, no chance of gain) are insurable — for example death, illness, accident, fire.', k: 'peril hazard', r: ['Insurable interest', 'Underwriting'] },
    { t: 'Pooling of risk', c: 'Core principles', d: 'The basic idea of insurance: many people pay small premiums into a common pool, and the pool pays the few who suffer a loss. The larger and more varied the pool, the more predictable the claims.', k: 'law of large numbers', r: ['Premium', 'Actuary'] },

    /* ---------- People & parties ---------- */
    { t: 'Policyholder', c: 'People & parties', d: 'The person who owns the policy and has the contract with the insurer — usually pays the premiums and can make changes. May or may not be the life assured.', k: 'proposer owner insured', r: ['Life assured', 'Beneficiary'] },
    { t: 'Life assured', c: 'People & parties', d: 'The person whose life is covered — the policy pays out on their death (or a covered event happening to them). The illustrator uses their age and gender to calculate premiums.', k: 'insured life covered person', r: ['Policyholder', 'Age next birthday'] },
    { t: 'Beneficiary', c: 'People & parties', d: 'The person or people who receive the policy proceeds. Naming a beneficiary clearly helps the claim to be paid quickly and avoids disputes.', k: 'nominee recipient payee', r: ['Nominee', 'Policyholder', 'Assignment'] },
    { t: 'Nominee', c: 'People & parties', d: 'Someone named by the policyholder to receive benefits. A nominee may in some cases only collect proceeds on behalf of the rightful heirs — explain this to clients and confirm wording with the company.', r: ['Beneficiary'] },
    { t: 'Insurer', c: 'People & parties', d: 'The licensed insurance company that issues the policy and promises to pay valid claims. Also called the underwriter or carrier.', k: 'underwriter carrier company', r: ['Reinsurer', 'IRA'] },
    { t: 'Reinsurer', c: 'People & parties', d: 'A company that takes over part of the risk an insurer has written, so that one very large claim does not threaten the insurer. Clients usually never deal with the reinsurer.', r: ['Reinsurance', 'Insurer'] },
    { t: 'Reinsurance', c: 'People & parties', d: 'Insurance for insurers: the original insurer passes (cedes) part of its risk and premium to a reinsurer in return for sharing claims.', r: ['Reinsurer'] },
    { t: 'Agent', c: 'People & parties', d: 'A licensed individual who sells and services insurance on behalf of an insurer and is paid commission. In law the agent acts for the insurer, so what the agent says and does can bind it.', k: 'intermediary representative advisor', r: ['Broker', 'Commission', 'IRA'] },
    { t: 'Broker', c: 'People & parties', d: 'An intermediary who acts for the client, comparing products from several insurers. Unlike a tied agent, a broker is not limited to one company.', r: ['Agent', 'Intermediary'] },
    { t: 'Intermediary', c: 'People & parties', d: 'Any person between the client and the insurer — agents, brokers and bancassurance partners.', r: ['Agent', 'Broker'] },
    { t: 'Actuary', c: 'People & parties', d: 'A professional who uses statistics and mathematics to measure risk, set premiums and bonus rates, and ensure the insurer can meet its future promises.', r: ['Mortality', 'Premium'] },
    { t: 'Underwriter', c: 'People & parties', d: 'The person (or system) who assesses each application and decides whether to accept it, and on what terms and price.', r: ['Underwriting', 'Loading'] },
    { t: 'Claimant', c: 'People & parties', d: 'The person who makes a claim — usually the beneficiary or policyholder, or the legal representative of the estate.', r: ['Claim', 'Beneficiary'] },
    { t: 'Trustee', c: 'People & parties', d: 'A person who holds policy benefits on behalf of others (for example young children) under a trust, and must use them for those people’s benefit.', r: ['Beneficiary', 'Minor beneficiary'] },
    { t: 'Minor beneficiary', c: 'People & parties', d: 'A child named to receive benefits. A child cannot give a valid receipt, so proceeds are normally held or paid through a guardian or trustee until the child is an adult — ask the company how it handles this.', r: ['Trustee', 'Beneficiary'] },

    /* ---------- Premiums & payments ---------- */
    { t: 'Premium', c: 'Premiums & payments', d: 'The amount the client pays the insurer for cover. It depends mainly on age, gender, sum assured, term, product and the riders chosen.', k: 'contribution payment cost price', r: ['Sum assured', 'Premium term', 'Frequency'] },
    { t: 'Premium term', c: 'Premiums & payments', d: 'How many years premiums must be paid. It can be shorter than the policy term (for example pay for 10 years, covered for 20).', k: 'premium paying term PPT', r: ['Policy term', 'Premium'] },
    { t: 'Policy term', c: 'Premiums & payments', d: 'How long the policy runs, from start until maturity or the end of cover.', k: 'term duration period', r: ['Premium term', 'Maturity'] },
    { t: 'Frequency', c: 'Premiums & payments', d: 'How often premiums are paid: monthly, quarterly, half-yearly (semi-annual), yearly or a single once-off payment. Paying more often is convenient but costs slightly more in total over a year because of the instalment loading; each frequency also has a minimum premium.', k: 'payment mode instalment', r: ['Premium', 'Single premium', 'Instalment loading'] },
    { t: 'Instalment loading', c: 'Premiums & payments', d: 'The small extra charged when premiums are paid in instalments instead of once a year, to cover the cost of collecting and the lost interest.', r: ['Frequency'] },
    { t: 'Single premium', c: 'Premiums & payments', d: 'One lump-sum premium paid at the start instead of regular payments. Suitable for a client with a lump sum (a bonus, sale proceeds, a pension payout).', k: 'lump sum once off', r: ['Frequency', 'Premium'] },
    { t: 'Regular premium', c: 'Premiums & payments', d: 'Premiums paid at fixed intervals over the premium term — the common way of building a policy from income.', r: ['Frequency', 'Single premium'] },
    { t: 'Minimum premium', c: 'Premiums & payments', d: 'The lowest premium the insurer accepts for a product and payment frequency. Illustrations below the minimum cannot be issued.', r: ['Frequency', 'Premium'] },
    { t: 'Minimum sum assured', c: 'Premiums & payments', d: 'The lowest cover amount the insurer will write on a product. The illustrator flags amounts below it.', r: ['Sum assured'] },
    { t: 'Level premium', c: 'Premiums & payments', d: 'A premium that stays the same for the whole premium term, even though the risk of dying rises with age (the early years pay for the later years).', r: ['Premium'] },
    { t: 'Premium holiday', c: 'Premiums & payments', d: 'A permitted break from paying premiums on some savings policies, after which payments resume. Benefits may be reduced and cover may be affected. Availability depends on the product.', r: ['Paid-up policy', 'Lapse'] },
    { t: 'Grace period', c: 'Premiums & payments', d: 'The extra time after a premium is due during which cover continues even though the premium is unpaid. If it is not paid by the end of the grace period the policy may lapse. Length is stated in the policy.', r: ['Lapse', 'Reinstatement'] },
    { t: 'Premium waiver', c: 'Premiums & payments', d: 'A feature (usually a rider) where the insurer pays the premiums if the policyholder becomes disabled or ill as defined, so the policy stays in force.', k: 'WOP', r: ['Waiver of Premium (WOP)'] },
    { t: 'Tax relief on premiums', c: 'Premiums & payments', d: 'In Kenya, qualifying life insurance premiums can earn insurance relief against income tax for clients who pay tax to KRA. The illustrator uses 15% of the premium paid, with the relief capped at KES 60,000 of relief a year (KES 5,000 a month). Tax rules change — confirm the current rate and limits with KRA or the company before quoting a saving to a client.', k: 'KRA insurance relief income tax PAYE', r: ['Premium'] },
    { t: 'Annual premium equivalent (APE)', c: 'Premiums & payments', d: 'A sales-reporting measure: regular premiums for a year plus a tenth of any single premiums. It lets different products and payment modes be compared fairly.', k: 'APE production', r: ['Premium', 'Commission'] },

    /* ---------- Benefits & values ---------- */
    { t: 'Sum assured', c: 'Benefits & values', d: 'The guaranteed amount the insurer agrees to pay on death or maturity (the basic cover), before bonuses or rider benefits. Often shortened to SA.', k: 'face amount cover benefit SA', r: ['Death benefit', 'Maturity benefit', 'Bonus'] },
    { t: 'Death benefit', c: 'Benefits & values', d: 'The amount paid to the beneficiary if the life assured dies during the policy term. It can be the sum assured, plus bonuses, or a percentage — always check the product wording (some plans pay only part of the sum assured in specified years).', r: ['Sum assured', 'Beneficiary'] },
    { t: 'Maturity benefit', c: 'Benefits & values', d: 'The amount paid when an endowment-type policy reaches the end of its term and the life assured is alive: the sum assured plus any bonuses, or the accumulated fund.', k: 'maturity value payout', r: ['Endowment', 'Bonus', 'Survival benefit'] },
    { t: 'Survival benefit', c: 'Benefits & values', d: 'A payment made to the policyholder at set points during the policy because the life assured is still alive (for example the instalments of an anticipated endowment), rather than only at the end.', k: 'anticipated payment money-back', r: ['Maturity benefit', 'Anticipated endowment'] },
    { t: 'Bonus', c: 'Benefits & values', d: 'A share of the insurer’s profits added to a participating (with-profits) policy. Bonuses are not guaranteed except where the policy says so, and past bonuses do not guarantee future ones.', r: ['Reversionary bonus', 'Terminal bonus', 'Guaranteed bonus', 'Participating policy'] },
    { t: 'Reversionary bonus', c: 'Benefits & values', d: 'A bonus added each year to the sum assured. Once added it cannot be taken away and is paid at death or maturity.', k: 'annual bonus', r: ['Bonus', 'Terminal bonus'] },
    { t: 'Terminal bonus', c: 'Benefits & values', d: 'An extra bonus paid at maturity, death or surrender, depending on the insurer’s performance. It is not guaranteed and can change or be zero.', k: 'final bonus', r: ['Bonus', 'Reversionary bonus'] },
    { t: 'Guaranteed bonus', c: 'Benefits & values', d: 'A bonus the insurer commits to pay at a stated point, for example a percentage of the sum assured at the end of the term, regardless of how investments perform.', r: ['Bonus'] },
    { t: 'Projected bonus rate', c: 'Benefits & values', d: 'The assumed bonus rate used in an illustration to show what the policy might be worth. It is a projection for discussion, NOT a promise. Always say so to the client.', k: 'illustrated rate assumption', r: ['Bonus', 'Illustration'] },
    { t: 'Participating policy', c: 'Benefits & values', d: 'Also called a with-profits or par policy. The policyholder shares in the insurer’s surplus through bonuses. Non-participating (without-profit) policies pay only the guaranteed benefits.', k: 'with profits par', r: ['Bonus', 'Non-participating policy'] },
    { t: 'Non-participating policy', c: 'Benefits & values', d: 'A policy with fixed, guaranteed benefits and no bonuses, such as pure term cover.', k: 'without profit non-par', r: ['Participating policy'] },
    { t: 'Surrender value', c: 'Benefits & values', d: 'The amount paid if the policyholder cancels a savings policy before maturity. In the early years it is often less than the premiums paid, because of the costs of setting up the policy.', k: 'cash value early exit withdrawal', r: ['Surrender', 'Paid-up policy'] },
    { t: 'Paid-up value', c: 'Benefits & values', d: 'The reduced sum assured that remains if the client stops paying premiums on a policy that has built up value — the policy stays alive without further premiums.', r: ['Paid-up policy', 'Surrender value'] },
    { t: 'Guaranteed benefit', c: 'Benefits & values', d: 'A benefit the insurer must pay if the conditions are met, whatever happens in the market. Contrast with projected or non-guaranteed amounts.', r: ['Projected bonus rate'] },
    { t: 'Illustration', c: 'Benefits & values', d: 'A document showing how a policy is expected to work for a particular client under stated assumptions. It is a sales aid for discussion, not a contract, offer or guarantee.', k: 'quotation quote projection', r: ['Projected bonus rate'] },
    { t: 'Compound annual growth rate (CAGR)', c: 'Benefits & values', d: 'The steady yearly rate that would take an amount from its starting value to its ending value over the period. Used to compare the growth of different plans on a like-for-like basis.', k: 'CAGR growth rate return', r: ['Projected bonus rate'] },
    { t: 'Discounting', c: 'Benefits & values', d: 'Working out today’s value of money to be received in future. A shilling today is worth more than a shilling in ten years.', k: 'present value', r: ['Compound annual growth rate (CAGR)'] },

    /* ---------- Policy life-cycle ---------- */
    { t: 'Proposal form', c: 'Policy life-cycle', d: 'The application the client completes (with the agent’s help) giving personal, health and financial details. The answers form the basis of the contract, so they must be complete and true.', k: 'application', r: ['Utmost good faith', 'Underwriting'] },
    { t: 'Policy document', c: 'Policy life-cycle', d: 'The written contract: schedule of benefits, premiums, terms, conditions and exclusions. Clients should read it and keep it safe.', k: 'policy schedule contract', r: ['Endorsement', 'Exclusion'] },
    { t: 'Commencement date', c: 'Policy life-cycle', d: 'The date cover starts. Premium due dates, waiting periods and the contestability period are counted from it.', k: 'start date inception effective date', r: ['Waiting period', 'Maturity'] },
    { t: 'Maturity', c: 'Policy life-cycle', d: 'The end of the policy term, when an endowment-type policy pays its maturity benefit and cover ends.', k: 'maturity date expiry', r: ['Maturity benefit', 'Policy term'] },
    { t: 'Free-look period', c: 'Policy life-cycle', d: 'A short window after the policy is issued during which the client may cancel and get the premium back. The length and conditions are set in the policy documents — always tell the client where to find them.', k: 'cooling off cancellation', r: ['Policy document'] },
    { t: 'Lapse', c: 'Policy life-cycle', d: 'The policy ends because premiums were not paid within the grace period. Cover stops and any value may be lost or reduced. A lapsed policy may sometimes be reinstated.', k: 'default non-payment', r: ['Grace period', 'Reinstatement', 'Persistency'] },
    { t: 'Reinstatement', c: 'Policy life-cycle', d: 'Bringing a lapsed policy back into force, usually by paying the missed premiums and sometimes proving good health again, within a stated period.', k: 'revival', r: ['Lapse'] },
    { t: 'Paid-up policy', c: 'Policy life-cycle', d: 'A policy where premiums have stopped but the policy continues with a reduced benefit, instead of lapsing or being surrendered.', r: ['Paid-up value', 'Lapse'] },
    { t: 'Surrender', c: 'Policy life-cycle', d: 'Voluntarily cancelling a policy and taking its surrender value. Usually a poor choice in the early years; explore alternatives (premium holiday, paid-up) first.', r: ['Surrender value'] },
    { t: 'Policy loan', c: 'Policy life-cycle', d: 'A loan against the surrender value of some savings policies, repaid with interest. If it is not repaid, the unpaid amount is deducted from the claim or surrender value.', r: ['Surrender value'] },
    { t: 'Endorsement', c: 'Policy life-cycle', d: 'A written change to the policy — for example a new address, beneficiary or extra cover — issued by the insurer and attached to the policy.', k: 'amendment alteration', r: ['Policy document'] },
    { t: 'Assignment', c: 'Policy life-cycle', d: 'Transferring the rights in a policy to someone else, for example to a bank as security for a loan.', r: ['Beneficiary', 'Policyholder'] },
    { t: 'Conversion', c: 'Policy life-cycle', d: 'Changing one policy into another type, such as term cover into a savings plan, often without fresh medical evidence if done in time. Check the policy terms.', r: ['Term assurance'] },
    { t: 'Persistency', c: 'Policy life-cycle', d: 'The share of policies that stay in force from year to year. High persistency means clients keep paying; low persistency (many lapses) hurts clients and the business.', k: 'retention lapse ratio', r: ['Lapse'] },
    { t: 'Waiting period', c: 'Policy life-cycle', d: 'A period after the policy starts (or after a rider is added) during which certain benefits cannot yet be claimed, for example illness cover.', k: 'qualifying period', r: ['Commencement date', 'Exclusion'] },

    /* ---------- Riders & extra covers ---------- */
    { t: 'Rider', c: 'Riders & extra covers', d: 'An optional extra benefit attached to a base policy for an extra premium — for example accident cover, disability cover or a funeral benefit. A rider ends when the base policy ends.', k: 'add-on supplementary benefit extra', r: ['Accidental Death Benefit', 'Waiver of Premium (WOP)', 'Total & Permanent Disability (TPD)'] },
    { t: 'Accidental Death Benefit', c: 'Riders & extra covers', d: 'A rider paying an additional sum if the life assured dies because of an accident, as defined in the policy.', k: 'ADB double indemnity accident', r: ['Rider', 'Proximate cause'] },
    { t: 'Total & Permanent Disability (TPD)', c: 'Riders & extra covers', d: 'A rider paying a benefit if the life assured becomes totally and permanently disabled and can no longer work, as defined in the policy (the definition and proof required are important — read the wording).', k: 'TPD disability', r: ['Rider', 'Waiver of Premium (WOP)'] },
    { t: 'Waiver of Premium (WOP)', c: 'Riders & extra covers', d: 'A rider under which the insurer pays future premiums if the policyholder suffers a defined disability or event, so the policy continues and the goal (for example school fees) is not lost.', k: 'WOP premium waiver', r: ['Rider', 'Premium waiver'] },
    { t: 'Accident hospitalisation rider', c: 'Riders & extra covers', d: 'A rider that pays a benefit if the life assured (adult) or the insured child is admitted to hospital because of an accident. Limits and exclusions apply.', k: 'AAHR CAHR hospital accident adult child', r: ['Rider'] },
    { t: 'Last expense rider', c: 'Riders & extra covers', d: 'A rider that pays a lump sum quickly on the death of the life assured (or, where offered, a covered child) to meet funeral and immediate costs while the main claim is being processed.', k: 'funeral cover last expense LE', r: ['Rider', 'Sum assured'] },
    { t: 'Whole Life Cover rider', c: 'Riders & extra covers', d: 'Cover that continues for life (not just for a term) and pays out whenever the life assured dies, as long as the rider is in force and premiums are paid. In the illustrator it is offered with Faida Maisha within a stated sum-assured range.', k: 'WLC lifelong', r: ['Whole life', 'Rider'] },
    { t: 'Critical illness cover', c: 'Riders & extra covers', d: 'Pays a lump sum if the life assured is diagnosed with a listed serious illness (such as cancer, heart attack, stroke). Only the illnesses listed in the policy are covered, and definitions are strict.', k: 'dread disease CI', r: ['Rider', 'Exclusion'] },
    { t: 'Hospital cash', c: 'Riders & extra covers', d: 'A fixed daily amount paid for each night in hospital, regardless of the actual bill. It supplements — does not replace — medical insurance.', r: ['Rider'] },

    /* ---------- Underwriting & claims ---------- */
    { t: 'Underwriting', c: 'Underwriting & claims', d: 'The process of assessing a risk — checking age, health, occupation, lifestyle and financial need — to decide whether to accept it and at what price.', k: 'risk assessment', r: ['Loading', 'Medical underwriting', 'Material fact'] },
    { t: 'Medical underwriting', c: 'Underwriting & claims', d: 'Underwriting that uses health questions and sometimes medical tests or reports. Higher sums assured or older ages usually need more evidence.', k: 'medical exam tests', r: ['Underwriting'] },
    { t: 'Loading', c: 'Underwriting & claims', d: 'An extra charge added to the premium for a higher-than-average risk (for example a health condition or dangerous occupation). Also used for the cost of paying in instalments.', k: 'extra premium rated up', r: ['Underwriting', 'Premium'] },
    { t: 'Exclusion', c: 'Underwriting & claims', d: 'A situation the policy will not pay for. Common examples are suicide within a stated period, war, self-inflicted injury, criminal acts, and pre-existing conditions. Always point out the main exclusions to the client.', k: 'not covered limitation', r: ['Pre-existing condition', 'Proximate cause'] },
    { t: 'Pre-existing condition', c: 'Underwriting & claims', d: 'A health condition the client had before the policy started. It must be disclosed and may be excluded or priced higher.', r: ['Exclusion', 'Non-disclosure'] },
    { t: 'Contestability period', c: 'Underwriting & claims', d: 'An initial period (often the first one or two years) during which the insurer can look closely at the proposal and may reject a claim if material facts were not disclosed. Check the policy for the exact rule.', k: 'incontestability', r: ['Non-disclosure'] },
    { t: 'Claim', c: 'Underwriting & claims', d: 'A request to the insurer to pay a benefit under the policy. To be paid it must fall within the cover, the policy must be in force and the documents requested must be supplied.', k: 'claims process notification', r: ['Claimant', 'Exclusion', 'Proximate cause'] },
    { t: 'Claims documents', c: 'Underwriting & claims', d: 'Typically the claim form, the original policy document, ID of the claimant, the death certificate and burial permit (for death claims), and any medical or police reports the insurer requires. Ask the company for its current checklist.', k: 'death certificate burial permit documents required', r: ['Claim'] },
    { t: 'Excess', c: 'Underwriting & claims', d: 'The portion of a loss the insured pays before the insurer pays (also called a deductible). Common in general and medical insurance rather than life assurance.', k: 'deductible', r: ['Co-insurance'] },
    { t: 'Co-insurance', c: 'Underwriting & claims', d: 'Sharing a risk between insurers, or the client paying a percentage of each claim (co-payment).', k: 'co-payment copay', r: ['Excess'] },
    { t: 'Mortality', c: 'Underwriting & claims', d: 'The rate of death in a group of people of a given age and gender. Mortality tables drive life-insurance prices.', k: 'death rate qx', r: ['Actuary', 'Premium'] },
    { t: 'Morbidity', c: 'Underwriting & claims', d: 'The rate of illness or disability in a group. Used to price health and disability benefits.', r: ['Mortality'] },
    { t: 'Fraud', c: 'Underwriting & claims', d: 'Deliberately giving false information or staging a loss to obtain a benefit. It makes the policy void, can lead to prosecution, and agents must never take part in or ignore it.', r: ['Misrepresentation'] },

    /* ---------- Types of life cover ---------- */
    { t: 'Life assurance', c: 'Types of life cover', d: 'Cover that pays an agreed sum on death (and, for savings types, on survival to a date). "Assurance" is used for events that are certain to happen (death) and "insurance" for events that may happen — in practice the words are used interchangeably.', k: 'life insurance', r: ['Term assurance', 'Whole life', 'Endowment'] },
    { t: 'Term assurance', c: 'Types of life cover', d: 'Pure protection for a fixed period: pays the sum assured only if the life assured dies within the term. No savings or maturity value, so premiums are the lowest for the amount of cover.', k: 'term insurance pure protection', r: ['Whole life', 'Endowment'] },
    { t: 'Whole life', c: 'Types of life cover', d: 'Cover that lasts the whole of life and pays the sum assured whenever death occurs. Some versions build a cash value and may share in profits.', k: 'whole assurance lifetime', r: ['Term assurance', 'Whole Life Cover rider'] },
    { t: 'Endowment', c: 'Types of life cover', d: 'A savings-and-protection policy that pays the sum assured (plus bonuses) at the end of the term if the life assured is alive, or on earlier death. Good for goals with a date: school fees, retirement, a project.', k: 'savings plan maturity', r: ['Maturity benefit', 'Term assurance'] },
    { t: 'Anticipated endowment', c: 'Types of life cover', d: 'An endowment that pays part of the sum assured in instalments at set times during the term (money-back), with the balance at maturity.', k: 'money back survival benefit', r: ['Survival benefit', 'Endowment'] },
    { t: 'Joint life policy', c: 'Types of life cover', d: 'One policy covering two lives (for example spouses) that pays out on the first death (or, if stated, the second death).', k: 'joint maxlife', r: ['Life assured'] },
    { t: 'Mortgage protection', c: 'Types of life cover', d: 'Cover linked to a loan so that the debt is cleared if the borrower dies. The cover may reduce as the loan reduces.', k: 'credit life loan decreasing term', r: ['Term assurance'] },
    { t: 'Education plan', c: 'Types of life cover', d: 'A savings policy built to meet future school or university costs on dates the client chooses, usually with cover that makes sure the plan is completed if the parent dies or becomes disabled.', k: 'school fees child', r: ['Endowment', 'Waiver of Premium (WOP)'] },
    { t: 'Group life', c: 'Types of life cover', d: 'One policy covering many people, usually the employees of one employer, often as a multiple of salary, with simple underwriting.', k: 'employer staff', r: ['Life assurance'] },
    { t: 'Annuity', c: 'Types of life cover', d: 'A regular income paid for life or a fixed period, bought with a lump sum (often at retirement).', k: 'pension income retirement', r: ['Single premium'] },
    { t: 'Unit-linked', c: 'Types of life cover', d: 'A policy where premiums buy units in an investment fund, so benefits rise or fall with fund performance. Different from with-profits, where the insurer smooths bonuses.', r: ['Participating policy'] },

    /* ---------- Savings & investment ---------- */
    { t: 'Capital guarantee', c: 'Savings & investment', d: 'A promise that the amount invested will not fall below a stated figure if held to the end of the term. Check exactly what is guaranteed and when.', r: ['Guaranteed benefit'] },
    { t: 'Inflation', c: 'Savings & investment', d: 'The general rise in prices over time. A fixed sum assured buys less in future, so goals such as school fees should be set at future, not today’s, cost.', r: ['Discounting'] },
    { t: 'Compounding', c: 'Savings & investment', d: 'Earning returns on earlier returns, so growth speeds up over time. The earlier the client starts, the larger the effect.', r: ['Compound annual growth rate (CAGR)'] },
    { t: 'Risk and return', c: 'Savings & investment', d: 'Higher possible returns usually come with higher risk. The illustrator shows optimistic, realistic and cautious growth scenarios so clients see a range, not a single promise.', k: 'optimistic realistic pessimistic scenario', r: ['Projected bonus rate'] },
    { t: 'Liquidity', c: 'Savings & investment', d: 'How quickly and cheaply money can be turned into cash. Life policies are long-term and early exit can cost money, so clients should keep an emergency fund separately.', k: 'access emergency fund', r: ['Surrender value'] },
    { t: 'Partial withdrawal', c: 'Savings & investment', d: 'Taking out part of the policy value before maturity where the product allows it. Limits, charges and effects on cover are set in the product terms.', k: 'withdraw', r: ['Surrender value'] },

    /* ---------- Regulation & compliance ---------- */
    { t: 'IRA', c: 'Regulation & compliance', d: 'The Insurance Regulatory Authority — the Kenyan regulator that licenses insurers, agents and brokers and supervises the sector under the Insurance Act (Cap. 487). Agents must be properly registered/licensed and clients can complain to the IRA if a matter is not resolved by the company.', k: 'Insurance Regulatory Authority Insurance Act Cap 487 regulator licence', r: ['Agent', 'Policyholders Compensation Fund'] },
    { t: 'Policyholders Compensation Fund', c: 'Regulation & compliance', d: 'A fund established under the Insurance Act to protect policyholders (within limits) if a licensed insurer becomes insolvent. Ask the company or IRA for the current rules and limits before describing the protection to a client.', k: 'PCF insolvency', r: ['IRA'] },
    { t: 'KYC', c: 'Regulation & compliance', d: 'Know Your Customer: verifying who the client is (ID, KRA PIN, address, source of funds) before accepting business. Required by anti-money-laundering rules.', k: 'know your customer identification due diligence', r: ['AML'] },
    { t: 'AML', c: 'Regulation & compliance', d: 'Anti-money laundering: legal duties (including under the Proceeds of Crime and Anti-Money Laundering Act) to prevent insurance being used to launder money — know the client, watch for unusual or large single premiums, and report suspicious activity through the company’s channels.', k: 'money laundering POCAMLA suspicious transaction', r: ['KYC'] },
    { t: 'Data protection', c: 'Regulation & compliance', d: 'Client personal data must be collected only for a stated purpose, kept safe and not shared without a lawful basis (Kenya’s Data Protection Act, 2019). Do not store client details on your own devices, photograph IDs casually or share screenshots of illustrations that show personal details.', k: 'privacy personal data ODPC consent confidential', r: ['KYC'] },
    { t: 'Treating customers fairly', c: 'Regulation & compliance', d: 'The standard of conduct expected of agents: sell products suited to the client’s needs, explain clearly, disclose costs and exclusions, avoid pressure, and handle complaints properly.', k: 'conduct fair treatment suitability', r: ['Needs analysis', 'Mis-selling'] },
    { t: 'Complaint', c: 'Regulation & compliance', d: 'A client’s expression of dissatisfaction. Record it, pass it to the company promptly and do not argue or delay. If unresolved, clients can take it to the IRA.', k: 'grievance dispute', r: ['IRA'] },

    /* ---------- Selling & conduct ---------- */
    { t: 'Needs analysis', c: 'Selling & conduct', d: 'Understanding the client’s situation, goals and ability to pay before recommending a product: dependants, existing cover, savings habits, time horizon, and what must be protected. The illustrator’s Client Needs Diagnosis supports this.', k: 'fact find fact-finding needs diagnosis recommendation', r: ['Suitability', 'Treating customers fairly'] },
    { t: 'Suitability', c: 'Selling & conduct', d: 'A product is suitable when it matches the client’s needs, budget and understanding. The cheapest or highest-commission plan is not automatically the right one.', r: ['Needs analysis', 'Mis-selling'] },
    { t: 'Mis-selling', c: 'Selling & conduct', d: 'Selling a product that does not suit the client, or selling it with misleading information (for example presenting projected bonuses as guaranteed, or calling a savings plan "free money").', r: ['Suitability', 'Treating customers fairly'] },
    { t: 'Commission', c: 'Selling & conduct', d: 'The payment an agent earns from the insurer for selling and servicing a policy. It must never drive recommendations; if asked, be honest that the agent is paid by the company.', k: 'agent pay earnings', r: ['Agent', 'Persistency'] },
    { t: 'Objection handling', c: 'Selling & conduct', d: 'Responding to a client’s concerns (cost, trust, timing) by listening, clarifying the real worry and answering truthfully with facts from the product documents. Never argue; never make promises the policy does not make.', k: 'objections concerns', r: ['Needs analysis'] },
    { t: 'Age next birthday', c: 'Selling & conduct', d: 'The age used for pricing: the client’s age at their next birthday. A person aged 34 years and 8 months is 35 next birthday. Premium and eligibility depend on it, so always confirm the date of birth.', k: 'ANB entry age', r: ['Life assured'] },
    { t: 'Needs gap', c: 'Selling & conduct', d: 'The difference between the cover a client needs (to protect dependants, clear debts, fund goals) and what they already have. The diagnosis helps identify the gap.', k: 'shortfall protection gap', r: ['Needs analysis'] }
  ],

  HANDBOOK: [
    { t: 'Your daily spark', c: 'Using the app', h: '<p>The first time you open the app each day, a greeting card appears with one motivational quote. Read it and tap <b>Let’s go</b> (or the ×). It appears only once a day, and you will not see the same quote again until you have been through the whole library of over 1,000. Tap <b>Today’s spark</b> on the home screen to read it again, or <b>Share</b> to send it to a friend or your team on WhatsApp.</p>' },
    { t: 'Sending an application from a quote', c: 'Selling & conduct', h: '<p>After you run an illustration, tap <b>Start application</b>. The plan, premium and frequency are filled in for you. Then follow six short steps: <b>client details</b> (name, date of birth, ID, KRA PIN, phone), <b>plan</b>, <b>beneficiaries</b> (shares must add up to 100%; a child under 18 needs a named trustee or guardian), <b>health declaration</b>, <b>documents</b> (photos of ID front and back and KRA PIN; photos are shrunk automatically) and <b>sign &amp; send</b> (the client agrees to the data consent and signs with a finger).</p><p>If you have no signal the application is kept safely on the phone and sent by itself when data returns. The home screen shows how many items are <i>waiting to upload</i>. If the office asks for more information, the application reappears under <b>Applications</b> with their note.</p><p>After sending you can collect the first premium: either the paybill instructions are shown, or (if your company has switched it on) an M-Pesa prompt is sent to the client’s phone. Never ask for the client’s M-Pesa PIN yourself.</p>' },
    { t: 'Client view and Kiswahili', c: 'Selling & conduct', h: '<p>On a result screen tap <b>Client view</b> to show the client a large-print page with two boxes: <b>what you pay</b> and <b>what you get</b>. Switch between English and Kiswahili, and make the text larger or smaller. It repeats that the figures are an illustration and not a promise. The home screen also has a <b>Text size</b> button that makes the whole app larger for easier reading.</p>' },
    { t: 'Renewals, lapsed policies and complaints', c: 'Selling & conduct', h: '<p><b>Policies &amp; renewals</b> lists your clients’ policies with premiums that are overdue, due in the next 14 days or lapsed. Tap Call, SMS or WhatsApp; a polite reminder in English or Kiswahili is prepared for you. Keeping existing clients paid up is usually worth more than finding new ones.</p><p>If a client is unhappy, use <b>Complaints</b> to write it down. It goes to the company, which must respond within its set period. Never discourage a client from complaining. The <b>Needs &amp; suitability</b> record, signed by the client, shows why the product you recommended suits them.</p>' },
    { t: 'Your licence and the knowledge quiz', c: 'Conduct & compliance', h: '<p>Your company may require a valid IRA licence number and a passed knowledge quiz before you can send applications. Enter the licence number and expiry date under <b>Licence &amp; training</b>; you will be warned before it expires. The quiz has 10 questions on fair selling and good practice; review the explanations for anything you get wrong. The office checks licence numbers against the IRA register.</p>' },
    { t: 'Client tools: records, sharing, comparing', c: 'Selling & conduct', h: '<p><b>My clients</b> (home screen) keeps names, follow-up dates and saved quotes on this phone only. On any result screen use <b>Save to client</b>, <b>Share with client</b> (WhatsApp, SMS, copy or a PDF file), <b>Add to comparison</b> (up to three quotes side by side) and <b>What-if</b> (move the premium, term or assumed bonus rate and watch the figures change; nothing is saved or counted).</p><p>The <b>cover gap</b> and <b>goal gap</b> charts on the diagnosis are quick rules of thumb, not a full needs analysis. <b>Why this plan?</b> shows which of the client\'s answers pushed a product up or down.</p><p><b>Hand to sales team</b> sends a client\'s name and contact to the company only when the client has agreed to be contacted. A shared summary never replaces the official printed illustration the client signs.</p><p><b>Training mode</b> lets you practise with sample clients; nothing is recorded or sent and printouts are marked as training.</p>' },
    { t: 'How to use the illustrator, step by step', c: 'Using the app', h: '<ol><li><b>Start</b> from the home screen: choose <i>Client Needs Diagnosis</i> if the client is unsure which plan to take, or <i>Go straight to the Illustrator</i> if the plan is already chosen.</li><li><b>Pick the product family</b> (Traditional &amp; Endowment, Faida, Smart Save/Scholar) and the specific plan.</li><li><b>Enter the client’s details</b>: date of birth (the app works out age next birthday), gender, term, premium frequency and either the premium they can afford, the sum assured they want, or the target amount.</li><li><b>Add riders</b> only where they fit the client’s needs and are offered on that plan.</li><li><b>Review the illustration</b>, explain the guaranteed and projected figures, then <b>Print / Save as PDF</b>.</li></ol><p>Use <b>Back</b> to change an earlier answer and <b>Start over</b> for a new client. Client details are not stored by the app; they disappear when you start over or sign out.</p>' },
    { t: 'Client Needs Diagnosis — what it does', c: 'Using the app', h: '<p>A short guided questionnaire (age, dependants, goals, time horizon, savings habits, liquidity needs, existing cover and investments, marital status) that scores each product against the client’s needs and shows the strongest fits with reasons. It then runs the real calculation for each product side by side so options can be compared fairly.</p><p><b>It is a guide, not advice or approval.</b> It does not replace full fact-finding, affordability checks or the company’s official illustration.</p>' },
    { t: 'Reading an illustration with a client', c: 'Using the app', h: '<ul><li><b>Guaranteed</b> figures are what the policy promises. <b>Projected</b> figures use an assumed bonus or growth rate and may be higher or lower.</li><li>Show the <b>death benefit</b> and the <b>maturity benefit</b> separately — they answer different needs (protection vs savings).</li><li>Point out the <b>premium term</b> versus <b>policy term</b>, the payment frequency, and the effect of <b>riders</b> on the premium.</li><li>Explain that <b>early surrender</b> normally returns less than was paid in the first years.</li><li>Say clearly that the illustration is <b>not an offer, quotation or policy</b>.</li></ul>' },
    { t: 'Printing, signing out and sharing devices', c: 'Using the app', h: '<ul><li><b>Print / Save as PDF</b> sits at the end of every illustration. The printed page carries your name, agency code, phone and email.</li><li>If someone else will use the phone, tap the round <b>account button</b> (your initials, top right) and choose <b>Sign out</b>. This clears your login and any client figures so the next person must sign in with their own details.</li><li>Use <b>Edit my details</b> to correct your name, agency code or email. A verified phone number can only be changed by signing in again with the new number.</li></ul>' },
    { t: 'Updates and when the app will not open', c: 'Using the app', h: '<p>The app checks for updates every time it is opened with internet, and regularly while in use. When a newer version exists, an <b>Update required</b> screen appears and the app will not open until you tap <b>Install update</b>. It then reopens by itself.</p><p>If the update does not finish, connect to the internet and try again. The app must also go online at least once in a while (the period set by your administrator) to keep working.</p>' },
    { t: 'Your responsibilities as an agent (from the Terms of Use)', c: 'Conduct & compliance', h: '<ul><li>You act as an authorised agent on your own responsibility.</li><li>Every figure you enter is your responsibility to verify before relying on it or sharing it.</li><li>An illustration is for discussion only and is <b>not a binding offer, quotation or policy document</b> unless separately confirmed through official channels.</li><li>You are responsible for how you use, print, share or distribute any document the tool produces.</li><li>Your name, agency code, phone and email are recorded on this device and on illustrations; when the app is connected to the administrator service, they are shared with the system administrators together with how you use the app. <b>Client information you type is not collected.</b></li></ul>' },
    { t: 'Before you sell: a short checklist', c: 'Conduct & compliance', h: '<ol><li>Confirm identity (KYC) and date of birth.</li><li>Understand the client’s needs, dependants, existing cover and budget.</li><li>Check eligibility: age, term, minimum premium and minimum sum assured for the plan.</li><li>Explain benefits, <b>exclusions</b>, waiting periods and what happens if premiums stop.</li><li>Be clear which figures are guaranteed and which are projections.</li><li>Make sure the client discloses every material fact on the proposal form.</li><li>Record the beneficiary correctly and keep copies of what was signed.</li><li>Give the client the company’s official documents and explain the free-look period.</li></ol>' },
    { t: 'Things you must never do', c: 'Conduct & compliance', h: '<ul><li>Never promise returns or present projected bonuses as guaranteed.</li><li>Never advise a client to hide or change medical or other facts.</li><li>Never accept cash or payments on the company’s behalf outside its approved channels.</li><li>Never take or pass on a client’s personal details casually (photos of ID, screenshots, group chats).</li><li>Never advise a client to cancel an existing policy to buy a new one without a full comparison of the costs and benefits.</li><li>Never sell under pressure or to someone who does not understand the product.</li></ul>' },
    { t: 'Quick comparison: which type of cover for which need', c: 'Choosing a product', h: '<table class="help-table"><thead><tr><th>Client need</th><th>Usually points to</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Protect family if the client dies, lowest cost</td><td>Term assurance</td></tr><tr><td>Cover for life, for estate and funeral needs</td><td>Whole life / Whole Life Cover rider</td></tr><tr><td>Save towards a dated goal with protection</td><td>Endowment (e.g. Fanaka, Career Life, Smart Save)</td></tr><tr><td>Fund a child’s education on set dates</td><td>Education plan (e.g. Smart Scholar, Faida Elimu)</td></tr><tr><td>Money at intervals during the term</td><td>Anticipated endowment</td></tr><tr><td>Invest a lump sum with cover</td><td>Single-premium plan</td></tr><tr><td>Protect a loan</td><td>Mortgage protection</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="help-note">This is a starting point only. Use the Client Needs Diagnosis and the Products tab for the specific plan rules.</p>' },
    { t: 'Company contacts (as printed on the illustrations)', c: 'Contacts', h: '<p><b>Jubilee Life Insurance Limited</b><br>Head Office: Jubilee Insurance HQ, Kilimanjaro Avenue, Upper Hill<br>P.O. Box 30376 – 00100 GPO, Nairobi, Kenya<br>Tel: 0709 949 000 / 328 1000<br>Email: talk2uslife@jubileekenya.com<br>Mombasa: Jubilee Insurance Building, Moi Avenue, P.O. Box 90220 – 80100 · Tel 222 4286 / 231 4019 / 231 6760</p><p class="help-note">Contact details come from the company’s illustration templates and may change; confirm with your office if a number does not work. To report a problem with this app or ask for a missing term, use the <b>Feedback</b> button.</p>' }
  ]
};
